  "title": "An introduction to TIPS and US CPI derivatives (2026 update)",
  "type": "strategy",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-07-22",
  "insights": [
   "The TIPS market remains the world's largest inflation-linked bond market with $2.2tn outstanding, though liquidity remains lower than nominal Treasuries with daily turnover averaging ~1%.",
   "Gross TIPS issuance is forecast at $235bn for 2026, with the TIPS share of total outstanding debt expected to decline to 6.6%, below the TBAC's 7-9% optimal range.",
   "Private investors have become the dominant holders of TIPS; the Fed's share of the market is expected to continue declining as it shifts portfolio duration toward bills.",
   "US CPI inflation swap volumes have grown rapidly, averaging $11.6bn/day in 1H26 (46% of TIPS daily volume), up from $2.2bn/day in 2020.",
   "TIPS breakevens are driven by commodity prices, Fed policy expectations, risk appetite (VIX), and economist inflation expectations; the model shows breakevens widen as commodity prices rise but with diminishing returns.",
   "Seasonality in CPI significantly impacts TIPS pricing, with July TIPS typically trading rich relative to January and April maturities due to the indexation lag.",
   "The embedded inflation floor in TIPS is currently worth 0-5bp of yield, but becomes valuable when expected inflation is near zero, as seen in 2020."
