  "title": "Liquid Cooling: What does Google's Brazos mean for the broader CDU ecosystem?",
  "type": "sector",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-07-22",
  "insights": [
   "Google's 'Brazos' project is a new open-source reference design for Liquid-to-Air (L2A) Coolant Distribution Units (CDUs) with 60kW capacity, intended for OCP-compliant inference workloads in legacy data centers.",
   "Brazos is not a competing product manufactured by Google, but a specification for the vendor ecosystem (Vertiv, nVent, Boyd) to build; it is designed for DC power infrastructure rather than traditional AC.",
   "The 60kW capacity is insufficient for frontier training racks (~120kW) but fits well for inference, which is expected to grow faster than training through 2030.",
   "Mid-term risks include commoditization of the inference CDU ecosystem, as Brazos specs are easier to deliver than the previous 'Deschutes' standard, potentially pressuring margins for manufacturers.",
   "Bernstein maintains an Outperform rating on major CDU manufacturers, arguing that an innovation premium persists for flagship L2L training CDUs where technical excellence is critical.",
   "Anecdotal evidence suggests some hyperscalers and colos are pushing out deliveries due to project delays, potentially increasing the future demand for retrofit-friendly L2A solutions like Brazos."
